.page fsck 8 check and mend a FAT file system
.alias fsck.fat fsck.vfat

# SYNOPSIS
*fsck* [*-n* | *-a* | *-p* | *-y*] [*-F*] [*-v*] [*-q*] _DEVICE_
*fsck.fat* ...
*fsck.vfat* ...

# DESCRIPTION
`fsck` goes through the ledger of an unmounted FAT12, FAT16 or FAT32 volume: _DEVICE_ is a partition such as `/dev/sdb2` (the Human Village on a USB stick) or an image file. It reads the boot sector, both FAT copies, every directory and every cluster chain, and reports what does not add up: chains that run into free, bad or foreign clusters, clusters claimed by two files (cross-links), files whose size and chain disagree, entries that point outside the volume, broken long names, missing `.` and `..` entries, duplicate names, clusters that are allocated but belong to nothing (lost clusters), a FAT copy that lags behind the other, a stale FSInfo sector.

It refuses to touch a volume that is mounted, or a disk with a mounted partition (`umount` the mount point first; for the Human Village that is `umount /HumanVillage`), and it refuses anything that is not a FAT volume. Only root may open the disk (`sudo fsck ...`). The boot sector itself is never repaired: a volume whose boot sector is damaged is refused with the reason, and Windows (`chkdsk`) or Linux (`fsck.vfat`) have to look at it.

The kernel runs the same check, read-only, on the Human Village when the volume was not closed properly (the power went out, the stick was pulled out). When nothing is out of place it mounts the volume read-write; otherwise it mounts it read-only, says what is wrong (`dmesg`, `/proc/fatcheck`) and leaves the repair to `fsck`. With `hvfsck=safe` on the kernel command line (`cmdline.txt` on the EFI partition) it makes the repairs of `-a` itself first, on the volume that is still unmounted, and goes through the ledger again from the device: when nothing is out of place then, the volume is mounted read-write and clean. What needs `-y` is never touched by the kernel and stays with `fsck`, which is the only way to clear the dirty flag of a volume with that kind of damage.

Every repair is made one at a time, after the problem has been looked at once more, and the work goes on in passes until a pass finds nothing left to do: a repair can change what the next one has to do (a file that was cross-linked gets its own copy of the shared clusters before the other file gives back what lies behind its end), so the report says how many passes it took. Nothing is written until the first repair is decided on, and the dirty flag is cleared only when the last pass found nothing wrong.

# OPTIONS
@ *-n*
    Look only. Nothing is written, not even the dirty flag.
@ *-a*, *-p*
    Automatic: make the repairs that lose nothing, and leave everything else alone. They are: a FAT copy that differs is overwritten by the good one, FSInfo is rewritten, the media byte and the backup boot sector are restored, a file never claims more bytes than its chain holds, the clusters behind a file's end are given back (also when that part of the chain is broken), bytes after a directory's end are cleared, long-name entries that belong to no file are removed (a file whose long name does not match its short entry stays under the short name), `.` and `..` are put right, an entry that is an exact copy of another one is removed, a file that is cross-linked with another gets its own copy of the shared clusters, and lost chains are saved as files `FOUND.000/FILE0000.CHK`, `FILE0001.CHK`, ... in the root directory (the data is kept; look at them, keep what is yours, delete the rest). When the volume is in order afterwards, the dirty flag is cleared. What `-a` leaves alone is listed, and the exit status is 4: run it again with `-y` if you accept the cost.
@ *-y*
    Say yes to everything, also to what costs something: a chain is cut where it breaks (the file keeps what lies before the break, and what lay behind becomes a lost chain), an entry that points outside the volume or only into another file's clusters is removed or emptied, an entry that repeats a name is removed or renamed, an entry that stands where `.` and `..` belong is removed, a stray volume label is removed, a bad 8.3 name is mended.
@ *-F*
    Give lost chains back to the free pool instead of saving them as files.
@ *-v*
    Show warnings and notes as well (lower-case letters in 8.3 names, odd attribute bits, bad dates) and the sector size of the device.
@ *-q*
    Only the summary.

Without `-n`, `-a` or `-y`, `fsck` asks before every repair when it is run on a terminal (`y` yes, `n` no, `a` yes to all, `q` stop; anything else is a no, and a repair that was declined is not asked about again); otherwise it behaves like `-n` and says so.

# EXIT STATUS
0 when nothing is wrong, 1 when problems were found and all of them corrected, 4 when problems are left (with `-n`: when any were found), 8 when the check could not be done (cannot open the device, it is mounted, it is not a FAT volume, the device failed), 16 for a usage error, 32 when the questions were stopped with `q` (or the terminal went away); what was agreed to before that is written.

# EXAMPLES
    umount /HumanVillage
    fsck -n /dev/sdb2          # what is wrong?
    fsck -a /dev/sdb2          # mend what loses nothing
    fsck -y /dev/sdb2          # and everything else
    reboot

# FILES
`/proc/fatcheck` holds the kernel's report of the last volume it went through. `FOUND.000/FILEnnnn.CHK` in the root of the volume are the lost chains that `fsck` saved (up to `FILE9999.CHK`; a volume with more lost chains than that needs `-F`). Each file is a whole number of clusters, so it ends in a little padding; a chain that was the start of a lost directory holds that directory's entries.

# SEE ALSO
mount(8), umount(8), dmesg(1), lsblk(1)
